# TylerOS environment configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored and must never be committed.
# Verify the result with: pnpm check:env

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL — the only variable the application itself cannot run without.
#
# Any PostgreSQL 14+ reached by a standard connection string works. TylerOS is
# provider-agnostic: no hosting provider is referenced anywhere in the code.
#
# Option A — local server (needs Docker or a local PostgreSQL install).
#   docker compose up -d
#   The line below already matches docker-compose.yml.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://tyleros:tyleros@localhost:5432/tyleros"

# Option B — a remote database (no Docker, no local PostgreSQL, no admin rights).
#   Create a database with any managed PostgreSQL host and paste its connection
#   string here, replacing the line above. Most hosted PostgreSQL requires TLS,
#   which is the standard `sslmode` parameter rather than anything app-specific:
#
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@your-host.example.com:5432/tyleros?sslmode=require"
#
#   Treat the string as a credential. Do not paste it into a commit or an issue.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests need nothing here. `pnpm test` runs PostgreSQL in-process via PGlite.
# The browser smoke suite (`pnpm test:e2e`) does use DATABASE_URL, and writes to
# it — point it at a development database.

# TylerOS collects no telemetry. This switches off the framework's.
NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_PASSPHRASE / SESSION_SECRET — who is allowed to see this.
#
# With neither set, and only in development (NODE_ENV=development, which
# `pnpm dev` sets for you), TylerOS is open: every screen loads with no
# sign-in step, and the server console says so once. That is the default for
# working on localhost.
#
# Outside development an absent or weak value is a hard failure: the server
# starts, logs the problem once, and every screen except /login and the
# installable icons answers 503 until this is set. See docs/DECISIONS.md
# ADR 030.
#
# SESSION_SECRET signs the session cookie. At least 32 characters, and not a
# short phrase padded with repetition — generate one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# AUTH_PASSPHRASE is what you actually type to sign in, at least 16
# characters. Something you can type on a phone keyboard is a reasonable
# trade for a private, single-user application — this is not protecting a
# bank.
#
# SESSION_SECRET="paste the output of the command above"
# AUTH_PASSPHRASE="choose something only you know"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI suggestions — entirely optional, and off unless you set a key.
#
# With nothing below configured, TylerOS makes no network call to any model,
# writes no suggestion rows, and behaves exactly as it did in 0.4.1. That is the
# default and it is a supported way to run this application, not a degraded one.
#
# What turning it on does: after an item is captured, TylerOS may propose a
# kind, an existing project and existing tags for it. Proposals appear as dashed
# chips in the inbox and on the item page, and change nothing until you accept
# one. Anything you typed — @project, #tag, a date, a repeat — is never sent and
# never overridden. See docs/DECISIONS.md ADRs 026 and 027.
#
# This needs an Anthropic API key, which is a separate paid product from any
# Claude subscription. Create one at https://console.anthropic.com/ and treat it
# as a credential: .env is git-ignored, and it must never reach a commit.
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."
#
# The same key is used for optional capture suggestions and for a manual
# Miles AI Today briefing (Ask Miles for AI briefing on /runs). The briefing
# still requires an explicit ai_execution_profiles row; the key is never
# stored in the database. AI_SUGGESTIONS=off does not hide this key.

# Which model proposes. Defaults to claude-opus-5.
# One short request per capture, so this is the cost dial — a smaller model such
# as claude-haiku-4-5 is a reasonable choice for classification this narrow.
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL is NOT read; the variable is AI_MODEL.
# AI_MODEL="claude-opus-5"

# The off switch, for a machine that has a key but should not spend it.
# Any value other than "off" leaves suggestions enabled.
# AI_SUGGESTIONS="off"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNTIME_TOKEN — machine pollers only. Optional until you run a worker.
#
# Humans still use the passphrase cookie. A Python (or later Grok) process
# acting as Miles calls `/api/runtime` with this bearer token instead.
# Absent or weak: the machine API is off, even in open development — a
# worker must not ride the cookie exemption. At least 32 characters:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# RUNTIME_TOKEN="paste the output of the command above"
#
# Per-instance worker credentials are created with:
#   pnpm runtime:bootstrap -- --key home-desktop-python --name "Home Desktop Python" --role miles
# The token is printed once. Put it on the worker as TYLEROS_RUNTIME_CREDENTIAL.

# Calendar day for food/drink logs (IANA time zone; defaults to Tyler's Phoenix zone).
TYLEROS_TIME_ZONE=America/Phoenix
